se_20181031_R2lox_S02_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S02_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S02_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S03_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S03_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S03_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S04_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S04_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S04_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S05_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S05_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S05_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S06_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S06_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S06_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S07_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S07_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S07_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S08_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S08_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S08_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S09_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S09_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S09_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S10_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S10_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S10_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S11_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S11_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S11_r3.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S12_r1.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S12_r2.raw\nGriese_20181031_R2lox_S12_r3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nLCMS quantification of internal crosslink in WT and G68L mutant R2lox proteins from Geobacillus kaustophilus.\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nA heterobimetallic Mn/Fe cofactor is found in the R2 subunit of class Ic ribonucleotide reductases (R2c) and R2-like ligand-binding oxidases (R2lox). Although the protein-derived metal ligands are the same in both groups of proteins, the connectivity of the two metal ions and the chemistry each cofactor performs are different: in R2c, a one-electron oxidant, the Mn/Fe dimer is linked by two oxygen bridges (μ-oxo/μ-hydroxo), whereas in R2lox, a two-electron oxidant, it is linked by a single oxygen bridge (μ-hydroxo) and a fatty acid ligand. Here we identify a second coordination sphere residue which directs the divergent reactivity of the protein scaffold. The residue that directly precedes the N-terminal carboxylate metal ligand is conserved as a glycine within the R2lox group, but not in R2c. Mutation of the glycine to leucine converts the resting state R2lox cofactor into an R2c-like cofactor, a µ-oxo/µ-hydroxo bridged MnIII/FeIII dimer. This species has recently been observed as an intermediate of the oxygen activation reaction in wild-type R2lox, demonstrating that it is physiologically relevant. Cofactor maturation in R2c and R2lox therefore follows the same pathway, with structural and functional divergence of the two cofactor forms following oxygen activation. We also show that the leucine mutant no longer functions as a two-electron oxidant. Specifically the mass spectrometry data here deposited tracks the abundance of the cross-linked peptide AVIRAATVYNMIVE-AVTLD, which was used as surrogate marker for the abundance of the internal V72-Y162 ether cross-link, which is directly impacted by the metalation state of the protein as well as the residue present in position 68 (G or L). Taken together, our data demonstrate that the residue preceding the N-terminal metal ligand directs the cofactor’s reactivity towards one- or two-electron redox chemistry, presumably by setting the protonation state of the bridging oxygens and thereby perturbing the redox potential of the Mn ion.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe purified R2lox apoproteins (both WT and G68L mutant) were incubated with metal salts so as to generate four types of metalloprotein samples: WT_MnFe; WT_FeFe; G68L_MnFe; G68L_FeFe. The four protein types were produced in triplicates. Samples were kept in the dark at all times during sample preparation, which included SP3-based cleanup and digestion by Glu-C in phosphate buffer pH 7.6 (leading to high cleavage rates at the c-terminal side of both glutamate and aspartate residues). LCMS in triplicate technical runs (in addition to the biological triplicates of the four protein types: 3x3x4=36 runs) was carried out on a HPLC 1200 system (Agilent Technologies) with a 15 cm long C18 picofrit analytical column (100 µm internal diameter, 5 µm bead size, Nikkyo Technos Co., Tokyo, Japan) connected to a LTQ Orbitrap Velos Pro mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific). WT_MnFe samples comprise mass spectrometry raw files files named S01-S03, WT_FeFe correspond to S04-S06, G68L_MnFe to S07-S09 and finally G68L_FeFe to S10-S12.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nGeobacillus kaustophilus\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
